00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:23.210
to this did not keep pace

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.050
with the changes that we were
seeing in our requirements.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.270
- Let me translate that for you.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.307
What he was really saying
is we weren't really

00:11:29.307 --> 00:11:32.630
paying attention because you know, war.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.070
According to this court document,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.943
Steven eventually did have
to pay up $389 million.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:41.090
But let's be honest.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.434
He got a little slap on the
wrist, but the guy got rich

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:46.780
off the Afghan war, which
is why he's on this list.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:49.813
Next up is this guy, Eric Prince.

00:11:49.813 --> 00:11:52.605
This is the guy we talked
about at the beginning,

00:11:52.605 --> 00:11:53.580
who was like charging $6,000 bucks

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:55.520
to fly people out of Afghanistan.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.420
- I don't think any human
being who has a heart

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.500
and soul would support efforts to profit

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.730
off of people's agony and pain,

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:04.130
as they're trying to depart a country

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.100
and fearing for their lives.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.660
- He's an ex Navy seal who
inherited a bunch of money

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.960
and decided to start
his own private security

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.570
company called Blackwater.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:12.403
Remember what I said?

00:12:12.403 --> 00:12:15.650
Private security company
really means soldiers for hire.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:18.320
This guy, trained military
and police forces.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.950
In fact, he describes his
mission for Blackwater

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:24.670
as "trying to do for the
national security apparatus

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:27.580
what FedEx did for the postal service."

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.140
Privatize, make more efficient,

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.220
get rid of red tape, etcetera, etcetera.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:33.380
Kind of a scary thought, but okay.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.360
He got his first contract in 2000.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.370
It was a small contract, $204,000

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.920
to help train the Navy
for terrorist attacks.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:43.570
Then 9/11 happened.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:46.310
An absolute tragedy for
the American people,

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:49.290
but an incredible business opportunity

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.940
for Eric Prince and
his FedEx for the army.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:56.030
The U.S. was desperate
to mobilize their troops

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:57.810
for this new kind of warfare.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.730
So they turn to Blackwater to help them.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.300
The U.S. hired Blackwater

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.900
to come protect their CIA compounds

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.320
or to escort VIP diplomats.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:08.950
But wait, let's just get something clear.

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:10.500
What are these guys?

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.303
Are they troops or like military?

00:13:14.358 --> 00:13:15.260
Technically, no, they're not,

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.300
but they sure look like troops.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.670
I mean, look at these guys

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:21.534
but really these guys are just
like fancy security guards

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:25.770
that the government hires and
gives them security clearances

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.610
and sends them to war zones
so that they can operate

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:32.563
like soldiers under the
jurisdiction of a private company.

00:13:33.544 --> 00:13:35.829
Hmm, but they're just security guards.

00:13:35.829 --> 00:13:38.870
So they're not like
mercenaries or anything, right?

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.860
By 2007, the government
had given Blackwater

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:45.250
over a billion dollars
in federal contracts.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:48.600
Let me repeat, this little
company that provides

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:52.440
a private security force,
went from meager contracts

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.126
of $200,000 to help train
troops, to sending troops abroad

00:13:56.126 --> 00:13:58.280
to actually be in the war zone

00:13:58.280 --> 00:13:59.780
and getting a billion dollars worth

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:02.670
of taxpayer money within just a few years.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.400
As you can imagine, giving
guns and security clearances

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.400
to a private army might lead
to some thorny abuses of power.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:15.180
And in fact, in 2007, the guards
of Blackwater were in Iraq

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.500
in addition to Afghanistan,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.610
and they opened fire on unarmed civilians.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:21.110
- [Narrator] Indiscriminate shootings

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:25.210
by Blackwater guards, leading
to the deaths of civilians.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:26.920
- In a crowded public square,

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:29.410
after a car bomb had exploded a mile away,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:32.900
they killed 17 Iraqis and injured 20 more.

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.730
Blackwater lost their contracts.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.300
It was a huge scandal.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.620
It was another instance where
there was some accountability.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:42.720
And yet Eric Prince sold the
company and came out on top.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.130
Another guy who got really
rich off the Afghan war.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.380
Oh, and those security
guards who opened fire

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:51.883
on innocent civilians
were tried and convicted,

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.550
but then wait for it.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.600
They were completely
pardoned by Donald Trump.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:00.270
Do we have any faith in humanity?

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.950
Next up we've got these two guys.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.450
They're nicknamed The War Dogs.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.240
There's a movie about
them that I haven't seen

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:07.170
but I want to.

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:08.369
- Sorry, excuse me.

00:15:08.369 --> 00:15:09.202
- Kind of an emergency.

00:15:09.202 --> 00:15:10.035
- Sorry, don't worry.

00:15:10.035 --> 00:15:11.070
I have to go first, I'm American

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:12.250
- But really the best thing out there

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:13.722
is this Rolling Stone article.

00:15:13.722 --> 00:15:15.190
(upbeat dance music)

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.960
These guys were ages 25 and 21

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.707
when they started an ammunition company.

00:15:20.707 --> 00:15:21.810
(gun shot)

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:25.308
They bid the pentagon and won $300 million

00:15:25.308 --> 00:15:29.000
worth of tax payer money in contracts.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.190
And they were gonna supply
the Afghan National Army

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.460
with guns in ammunition.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:33.310
I mean, this is insane.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.590
These were like early 20 year old kids.

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:38.438
One of these guys, David was
just a few months earlier

00:15:38.438 --> 00:15:41.200
a massage therapist and then was like,

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.060
hm, I'm gonna have a
little change of career.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:45.442
I'm gonna go sell $300 million worth

00:15:45.442 --> 00:15:48.840
of guns and ammunition
to the federal government

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.290
so they can fight a war in Afghanistan.

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:52.530
These guys got super rich off of this.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:55.440
And soon we're living a fancy
life with really nice cars

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.980
and expensive apartments in like Miami.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:01.130
But what they were doing
here was buying ammo

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:04.480
from China and then selling
that to the U.S. government,

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:08.860
which is like a giant
no-no in arms like trading.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.440
They eventually got
caught, pleaded guilty.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:14.100
And one of the him was sentenced
to jail for four years.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.230
So this is a rare case
of like guys got rich

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:20.990
and then they got caught and
they actually got punished

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:24.070
which didn't happen to
Supreme Food or Eric Prince.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.758
The difference is like when
you're like a 24 year old kid

00:16:26.758 --> 00:16:28.620
you don't have a lot
of money and influence.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:31.340
So you can't like weasel your
way out of accountability.

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:34.410
But what this story shows you
is just how easy it was to get

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.670
in on this culture of endless
money within the Pentagon.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:39.760
The massage therapist
who had never owned a gun

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:43.380
before started an
ammunition and gun company

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:46.330
and got $300 million for
it, just like a gold rush.

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:47.860
Okay, so those are the War Dogs.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:51.330
Next up we have this guy, Fahim Hashimy,

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:55.160
one of the several Afghan
nationals who got super rich,

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:57.740
part of this group called
the 9/11 millionaires.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.110
In the early days of the Afghan war

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:03.730
there was huge need for
local fixers and translators.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:04.800
The people who got in early,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:07.260
helping out U.S. forces got really savvy

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:08.970
to how the U.S. was operating.

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:10.040
And they quickly realized

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.910
that they could provide a
bunch more local services

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.280
and make a to ton of money.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.790
So Fahim started a business
that eventually turned

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:18.440
into like a media corporation

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.120
with like TV stations and
a construction company

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:22.400
and even an airline.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.450
His company started racking in

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.430
like $200 million a year
in government contracts.

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:29.770
There were a bunch of
Afghans who got super rich

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:33.090
riding on the coattails of
all of this money being poured

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:36.540
into the country, which
it's like good for them.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.130
Their country's been torn apart

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:41.042
by outside superpowers for decades.

00:17:41.042 --> 00:17:44.650
Let them at least get something
out of it for heaven's sake.

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:49.252
Next up, we have our very own Congress.

00:17:49.252 --> 00:17:52.469
♪ Oh say can you see ♪

00:17:52.469 --> 00:17:53.740
♪ By the dawn's early light ♪

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.340
Nancy Pelosi and Ted Cruz

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:57.320
aren't like arms dealers,

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:00.073
but they can still get rich off of war.

00:18:00.073 --> 00:18:00.906
(bomb exploding)

00:18:00.906 --> 00:18:02.468
(cash register machine)

00:18:02.468 --> 00:18:03.301
- [Man] Everything is gone.

00:18:03.301 --> 00:18:05.183
- [Man] Oh my God.

00:18:05.183 --> 00:18:07.250
- Because if you're a member of Congress

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:10.510
you can buy stocks in defense companies

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:11.650
like Lockheed Martin,

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:12.983
and a lot of them do.

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:16.860
At least 47 members of Congress

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:21.860
and their spouses hold
between $2.67 million worth

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.263
of stocks in top defense companies.

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:28.120
Oh, and it's not partisan.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.870
Of the 30 house members who hold stock

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:32.130
and military contracts

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:35.620
20 are Republican and 16 are Democrats.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.010
It's almost split in half.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:38.540
I mean, let's get into the details here.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:42.400
Like which of the congressmen
have the most money invested

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:43.780
in defense companies?

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:45.050
Good question.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:46.460
The winner for this year

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.490
of who's getting rich
off war is Kevin Hern,

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:51.950
a Republican from Oklahoma who owns

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:53.980
over a million dollars in stock

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:55.839
from defense contractors.

00:18:55.839 --> 00:18:59.090
$880,000 alone in Honeywell,

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.342
which is an aerospace
contractor that Congress really

00:19:01.342 --> 00:19:05.200
seems to love and notice how
well their stock has done.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.840
Especially since 2009,
when Obama sent a bunch

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:10.750
of new troops to Afghanistan.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:13.225
I mean this is a lawmaker

00:19:13.225 --> 00:19:14.360
who has to give approval when we go

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:17.810
to war and he stands to gain if we do,

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:20.000
but wait the conflict
of interest gets worse,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.930
when you look at somebody
like Senator Gary Peters

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:25.020
a democratic Senator from Michigan

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.828
who is a member of the
Arms Services Committee,

00:19:27.828 --> 00:19:28.960
which is the committee

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.780
that authorizes the Pentagon's budget.

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:33.483
Here's his financial disclosure.

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:36.850
Hmm, he's invested in a lot of things

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:40.640
including Raytheon Technology Corporation.

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:43.810
Raytheon is a major government contractor

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:45.790
for war time things.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.380
This guy gives money

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:50.960
to the Pentagon to do war
things with private contractors

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:54.580
and he has invested in
the private contractors.

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:56.480
In July, this guy's committee voted

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:58.680
to increase defense spending

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:00.420
by $24 billion,

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.320
which is more than president
Biden even proposed.

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:04.520
So in very direct terms

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:07.102
this guy is voting to put
more money in his pocket.

00:20:07.102 --> 00:20:09.010
That is how our system works.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:11.990
Here's another financial
disclosure of a senator

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:13.440
who's on a committee that deals

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.480
with cyber security and protection.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.570
She holds stock in six defense companies

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:23.100
with most of her money being
invested in IT contractor.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:24.110
Do you get it?

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:26.260
IT contractor that she is invested in.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:27.610
Cyber security committee

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:30.280
that she uses to vote to give money

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:32.240
to the thing she's invested in.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.862
It's like, come on, ugh.

00:20:34.862 --> 00:20:37.340
(eerie music)

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.560
Okay, so that's our list.

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:42.328
And let me be clear that this
is not the exhaustive list

00:20:42.328 --> 00:20:43.580
of everyone who got rich.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.790
There are so many more
people who got rich.

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:49.010
There are so many more
people who abused the system.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.510
We will never know their names.

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:51.850
We will never hear about them

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.290
because they didn't get caught.

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:54.123
In the end,

00:20:54.123 --> 00:20:59.123
the Pentagon spent $14
trillion, $14 trillion

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:03.690
between 30% and 50% of that
went to private companies,

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:07.750
military contractors, companies
that give huge payout,

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:10.710
huge compensation to the top leaders.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:13.250
That's like $5 trillion worth

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:16.190
of taxpayer money going
to fatten the pockets

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.380
of these corporations and for what?

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:19.370
What did we get?

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:21.810
We now know that it didn't do much

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.820
for this country and its people.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:25.360
20 years later the country

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.510
is falling back to where it started.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.718
But what it did do is it made these people

00:21:30.718 --> 00:21:33.530
really, really, really rich.

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:34.363
Good for them.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:37.610
Okay, I'm gonna go try to not be depressed

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:38.810
about how our democracy works.

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:40.286
See ya.

00:21:40.286 --> 00:21:42.786
(eerie music)

